\section{Introduction}

AskewSGD is a new approach for neural network training on large-scale datasets, constructed by applying a specific transformation to the biased moment estimator found in Askew (Glasmair \& Pritzel, 2019). This methodology positions AskewSGD as a competitive general-purpose alternative to Adam (Kingma \& Ba, 2015). While AskewSGD exhibits a preference for specific neural network architectures and hyperparameter configurations, it offers superior performance, including faster convergence rates, across a broad spectrum of datasets and model architectures.

The methodology behind AskewSGD stems from the observation that standard estimators for covariance and lower-order moments fail to account for the mean, rendering them unsuitable for training algorithms based on score matching. Furthermore, these moments do not capture the full statistical characteristics of random variables, a deficiency that gradient-based neural network training methods depend upon. Instead, AskewSGD utilizes random variable covariates to compute unbiased estimators for higher-order moments, which are critical for effective long-tail training scenarios. This approach is derived from the asymmetric moment definition established by \cite{friz2008asymmetric}.

